  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 223 AM EST Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Record high temperatures for the date remain on track \nfor today; see Climate section of the forecast discussion for \ndetails. Progged 925 millibar temperatures of 16 to 18 Celsius, near \nclimatological maximum values, supports high temperatures in the low \nto mid 70s with dry adiabatic lapse rates across our valley \nlocations. The most unusually extreme temperatures are favored for \nthe Adirondacks and northeastern Vermont, where the Extreme Forecast \nIndex most recent run shows a high confidence of near record high \ntemperatures. This has trended more extreme with time, so we have \nused the 90th percentile temperature guidance for high temperatures. \nThe resulting forecast highs look consistent with recent \nverification of observed temperatures and for a scenario with \nsunshine and warm air advection from the southwest. Relative \nhumidity will remain fairly moderate based on guidance and observed \nvalues upstream in the Midwest, putting a likely minimum in the 40% \nrange. A few wind gusts may exceed 25 MPH this morning with \nchanneled flow in the Champlain Valley, but winds aloft generally \nlook too light to cause much concern even if boundary layer mixing \nis deeper than modeled.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A weakening cold front will put an end to our dry and \nvery warm weather. Two rounds of showers appears on the docket for \nThursday, with the first round being relatively disordered showers \ntied to a surge of anomalous moisture sliding northeastward through \nour region. This precipitation looks strongly forced with relatively \ndeep moisture such that rain chances will be nearly certain for at \nleast a brief period of time during the daytime hours. Towards \nevening, while moisture will be more limited, sufficiently steep \nlapse rates to yield a ribbon of instability and a chance of loosely \norganized showers that will track to the east across northern \nVermont Thursday night. As the front exits/washes out to our east, \nthink flow will be light enough amidst rich near surface moisture to \nsupport some patchy fog and drizzle regardless of how widespread \nthese evening showers wind up being. The lack of a wind shift will \nkeep temperatures and humidity above normal into Friday. However, \nconditions look mainly dry with our region sitting between an \ninverted trough to our east and an incoming upper level low to our \nwest. Most likely rainfall amounts being offered are mainly 0.1\" to \n0.33\", but with convective nature of some of the rain, footprints \nwill be quite variable.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Upper level troughing will likely settle over \neastern Canada and the Northeast as the longwave pattern is\nfavored to stall Saturday through the weekend. Model consensus \npoints to multiple rounds of rainfall associated with upper \nlevel low overhead and potential for surface low pressure to \ngyre and pivot back westward early next week while a series of\ntroughs move through the base of the longwave overhead. The\nresult will be for more shower chances Saturday onward with\ntemperatures cooling back into the 50s and upper 40s. Elevation\ndependent snowfall could occur with the cold pool expected to be\nover the region. It's feasible that there could be some lower\nelevation pellet showers should the troughs be more energetic \nas they move through. This pattern will favor cooler overnight \nlows as well with lower elevations in the 30s and mid/upper 20s \nabove 1500-2000ft through Monday. There are some indications \nthat reinforcing cold air may occur by Tuesday possibly pushing\ntemperatures lower Tuesday night.",
